From: Tamer Ahmed <tamerahmed@microsoft.com>
Date: Wed, 19 Aug 2026 18:30:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] zebra: allow a local MAC to be programmed into the kernel bridge FDB

zebra learns local MACs from the kernel, so it has never needed to program
one back. That assumption breaks for a dataplane that learns local MACs
somewhere else: with MAC synchronisation carried over FPM the switch ASIC
learns them and hands them to zebra directly, and nothing populates the
kernel bridge FDB for those MACs at all.

The kernel still needs them. Its ARP/ND state machine resolves against the
bridge FDB, so without an entry it keeps probing for a host the dataplane
can already reach.

Add kernel_upd_local_mac() and kernel_del_local_mac() for that case. The
entry is written with NTF_EXT_LEARNED so the bridge does not age something
it never learned, leaving ageing to whoever owns the port. No protocol is
set on it: the origin of a MAC now travels over the FPM channel, so tagging
the kernel entry would only duplicate that in a place nothing reads back,
and it would tie this path to a non-upstream kernel change.

An externally learned update is not enough on its own. The kernel takes a
separate path for NTF_EXT_LEARNED and ignores ndm_state there, so a NUD_NOARP
another writer already put on the entry survives the update and cannot be
cleared. zebra's own Ethernet Segment sync is such a writer, and on a zebra
restart it marks the entry static before the FPM channel is even connected.
Send the update a second time without NTF_EXT_LEARNED, a flag the entry keeps
once set, so the state is forced back. Correcting the entry in place rather
than deleting and re-adding it keeps the MAC forwarding throughout, so nothing
is flooded while it would otherwise be missing.

Nothing in zebra calls these yet; they are for a dataplane module to use, so
existing behaviour is unchanged.

Signed-off-by: Tamer Ahmed <tamerahmed@microsoft.com>
---
diff --git a/zebra/rt.h b/zebra/rt.h
index c501ff4530..5ea5dc4529 100644
--- a/zebra/rt.h
+++ b/zebra/rt.h
@@ -109,6 +109,18 @@ extern int kernel_upd_mac_nhg(uint32_t nhg_id, uint32_t nh_cnt,
 		struct nh_grp *nh_ids);
 extern int kernel_del_mac_nhg(uint32_t nhg_id);
 
+/*
+ * Program a locally learned MAC into the kernel bridge FDB.
+ *
+ * These exist for dataplanes that learn local MACs somewhere other than the
+ * kernel. Nothing else populates the bridge FDB for such a MAC, so without them
+ * the kernel keeps probing for a host it can already reach.
+ */
+extern int kernel_upd_local_mac(const struct interface *ifp, vlanid_t vid,
+				const struct ethaddr *mac, bool sticky);
+extern int kernel_del_local_mac(const struct interface *ifp, vlanid_t vid,
+				const struct ethaddr *mac);
+
 /*
  * FDB nexthop and nexthop-group programming does not build a dataplane
  * context, so a provider cannot observe it the way it observes a MAC install.
diff --git a/zebra/rt_netlink.c b/zebra/rt_netlink.c
index 6f958b5c27..7b69665312 100644
--- a/zebra/rt_netlink.c
+++ b/zebra/rt_netlink.c
@@ -4543,6 +4543,95 @@ ssize_t netlink_macfdb_update_ctx(struct zebra_dplane_ctx *ctx, void *data,
 	return total;
 }
 
+/*
+ * Write a locally learned MAC straight into the kernel bridge FDB.
+ *
+ * NTF_EXT_LEARNED stops the bridge ageing an entry it never learned itself,
+ * which is what keeps the kernel from probing a host the local dataplane can
+ * already reach. No protocol is set: where the MAC came from is carried on the
+ * FPM channel, and nothing reads it back off the kernel entry.
+ *
+ * ext_learn is cleared for the follow-up message that corrects ndm_state; see
+ * kernel_upd_local_mac().
+ */
+static int netlink_local_mac_update(int cmd, const struct interface *ifp,
+				    vlanid_t vid, const struct ethaddr *mac,
+				    bool sticky, bool ext_learn)
+{
+	struct {
+		struct nlmsghdr n;
+		struct ndmsg ndm;
+		char buf[256];
+	} req;
+	struct zebra_vrf *zvrf;
+	struct zebra_ns *zns;
+
+	zvrf = zebra_vrf_get_evpn();
+	if (zvrf == NULL || zvrf->zns == NULL)
+		return -1;
+	zns = zvrf->zns;
+
+	memset(&req, 0, sizeof(req));
+
+	req.n.nlmsg_len = NLMSG_LENGTH(sizeof(struct ndmsg));
+	req.n.nlmsg_flags = NLM_F_REQUEST;
+	if (cmd == RTM_NEWNEIGH)
+		req.n.nlmsg_flags |= NLM_F_CREATE | NLM_F_REPLACE;
+	req.n.nlmsg_type = cmd;
+	req.ndm.ndm_family = AF_BRIDGE;
+	req.ndm.ndm_state = NUD_REACHABLE;
+	req.ndm.ndm_flags = NTF_MASTER;
+	if (ext_learn)
+		req.ndm.ndm_flags |= NTF_EXT_LEARNED;
+	req.ndm.ndm_ifindex = ifp->ifindex;
+
+	if (sticky) {
+		req.ndm.ndm_flags |= NTF_STICKY;
+		req.ndm.ndm_state |= NUD_NOARP;
+	}
+
+	if (!nl_attr_put(&req.n, sizeof(req), NDA_LLADDR, mac->octet, ETH_ALEN))
+		return -1;
+	if (vid > 0 && !nl_attr_put16(&req.n, sizeof(req), NDA_VLAN, vid))
+		return -1;
+
+	if (IS_ZEBRA_DEBUG_KERNEL)
+		zlog_debug("Tx %s family %s IF %s(%u) VLAN %u MAC %pEA%s%s",
+			   nl_msg_type_to_str(cmd), nl_family_to_str(AF_BRIDGE),
+			   ifp->name, ifp->ifindex, vid, mac,
+			   sticky ? " sticky" : "",
+			   ext_learn ? "" : " state-only");
+
+	return netlink_talk(netlink_talk_filter, &req.n, &zns->netlink_cmd, zns,
+			    false);
+}
+
+int kernel_upd_local_mac(const struct interface *ifp, vlanid_t vid,
+			 const struct ethaddr *mac, bool sticky)
+{
+	int rv;
+
+	rv = netlink_local_mac_update(RTM_NEWNEIGH, ifp, vid, mac, sticky, true);
+	if (rv < 0 || sticky)
+		return rv;
+
+	/*
+	 * The kernel ignores ndm_state on an externally learned update, so a
+	 * NUD_NOARP another writer left on the entry outlives the message
+	 * above. Repeating it without NTF_EXT_LEARNED forces the state back
+	 * without removing the entry, so the MAC keeps forwarding.
+	 */
+	return netlink_local_mac_update(RTM_NEWNEIGH, ifp, vid, mac, sticky,
+					false);
+}
+
+int kernel_del_local_mac(const struct interface *ifp, vlanid_t vid,
+			 const struct ethaddr *mac)
+{
+	return netlink_local_mac_update(RTM_DELNEIGH, ifp, vid, mac, false,
+					true);
+}
+
 #define NUD_VALID                                                              \
 	(NUD_PERMANENT | NUD_NOARP | NUD_REACHABLE | NUD_PROBE | NUD_STALE     \
 	 | NUD_DELAY)
